From: Caboose <caboose@local>
Subject: [PATCH 57/57] Hold frees until the work before them completes; follow events and queries; fewer drains

With the completion feed, vkFreeMemory, vkDestroyBuffer, vkDestroyImage,
vkDestroyImageView and vkDestroyBufferView (and the parked views an image or buffer
destroy releases) are held until every queue submission sent before them has
completed on the phone (caboose-bounded-memory/deferred_destroys.h): work still
running on any queue can keep an object resident there, and releasing it under that
work loses the device. A queue whose latest submissions no completion watch follows
gets one when a destroy is held. Held destroys are sent after submits, idle waits and
before allocations, and all of a device's go before vkDestroyDevice; those of a
departed executor are never sent. A free releases its async-allocation budget when it
is sent.

Batches that end or reset queries are watched like those that set events. A no-wait
vkGetQueryPoolResults returns VK_NOT_READY without a request while the tracker knows
the result cannot be there (a request sent earlier reached the decoder before the GPU
had finished and was answered not ready, poll after poll); a range polled then is
asked for with the next submission after its batch completes. vkGetEventStatus of an
event the tracker does not follow sends its request without waiting and reports what
the latest answered request found (caboose-bounded-memory/event_status.h). Stalled
event and query polls name what the tracker knows of them.

vkBindBufferMemory2 and vkBindImageMemory2 (and their KHR aliases) no longer drain
before a deferred bind. With hello feature 0x10000 vkCreateBufferView uses a
guest-assigned handle and reads its reply later. Images and buffers made with the
GOOGLE create-with-requirements calls enter the view caches.

CABOOSE_READ_PROFILE names the calls whose opcodes are above 21009 instead of pooling
them as 21023, and adds the threads that waited longest locally (waiters=name:ms/n).
